## 3.2 Adsorption affinity
The porosity of Cu–BTC is an important point concerning the possible use of such functional materials for different purposes. For augment of porosity in Cu–BTC, we successfully tested its porosity with guest molecules by suspending it in an aqueous solution of Rif. The dark red solutions of Rif fade slowly to colorless (Figure 2A), while the Cu–BTC get darker. FT–IR for Cu–BTC containing Rif (KBr, $400{\mathrm{-}}4000~{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}.$ ): 3549 (s, broad), 3165 (s, broad), 1617 (s), 1578 (s), 1510 (s), 1438 (s), 1375 (s), 1092 (m), 801 (m), 750 (s), 729 (s), 593 (m), 533 (w) and 480 (m). We tested for the amount of Rif that can be inserted in the pores. The guest content was estimated by UV/vis spectroscopy (Figure 2B and Table 1). The change of intensity and width indicates that the resulting solid $\mathrm{Cu}$ –BTC retains the host framework crystallinity as Rif molecules diffused in [29, 30]. To explore the absorption ability of the Cu–BTC to rifampicin, a fresh sample of Cu– BTC ( $13.0~\mathrm{mg},$ ) was immersed in an aqueous $(50.0~\mathrm{mL})$ ) solution of Rif (0.17 mmol) and were monitored in real time with UV/vis spectroscopy. Results show that the sorption capacity of Cu–BTC increases with the increase of contact time (Figure 2B,C).
The Rif content is underestimated by UV/vis spectroscopy. As shown in Figure 2B, the adsorbed quantity of Rif over I (nano Cu–BTC) is much higher than those over an activated carbon and compound II (bulk Cu–BTC). In comparison as long as $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the uptake of Rif $(42.15\mathrm{m}\ \mathrm{g}/\mathrm{g})$ on I is remarkable and clearly exceeds that of II $(25.62\mathrm{mg/g)}$ and activated carbon $(18.85\mathrm{mg/g)}$ in the aqueous solution of Rif. In compound I and all of the nano–MOFs the channel length is decreased so that the amount of adsorption is increased a little. The skeleton of MOFs can sometimes be flexible [31], and Horcajada et al. reported a new class of dynamic hybrid solids which modulate their pore size upon adsorption of organic molecules [32,33]. This reversible “swelling” effect increases the cell volumes of the parent structure by $50\mathrm{-}230\%$ , depending on the structure and the length of the linker [33,34], without any apparent bond breaking. These compounds exhibit different adsorption behaviours as illustrated by different adsorption isotherm types (Fig.
2). The ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherm of compound $\mathbf{I}(376.4\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathbf{B}\mathrm{ET})$ and compound II (409.3 $4{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}{\mathrm{BET}})$ at $77\mathrm{K}$ displays a type I and VI sorption behaviour, respectively (Fig. 3).
