## 3.3 Release assays
The encapsulated guests could be easily removed from the frameworks upon immersion of guest $@$ MOFs (MOFs containing guest) in organic solvents. When the crystals of Rif upon Cu–BTC were soaked in dry ethanol, the color of the crystals changed gradually from gray to green in about $120\mathrm{~h~}$ , and the color of the ethanol solutions deepened gradually from colorless to darker red (Figure 4A). To further investigate the kinetics of the guest delivery of the framework, UV/vis spectra were recorded at room temperature. The temporal evolution UV/vis spectrum for Rif in ethanol solution becomes stronger with increasing Rif content (Figure 4B). The delivery of Rif in ethanol increases with time, indicating that the Rif release is governed by the host-guest interaction. Once these interactions fade out with increasing Rif extrusion, the delivery in the second stage is mainly governed by a free diffusion process, and a complete Rif release from I needs more than 7 days to attain the equilibrium state. However, Rif release from II and activated carbon occurs within tens of minutes and then moves toward the equilibrium state. The two parameters, the maximum Rif content and the rate, suggest that they may be due to a free diffusion process in the absence of well-defined host-guest interactions. It seems that in I the pores are more accessible than II, so the delivery of Rif from I can be faster [35,36]. A summary of the different treatments appears in Table 1.
