## 3.4 X-ray powder diffraction (XRPD)
To determine the crystal phase of products, XRPD measurements were carried out over the diffraction angle $(2\theta)$ of $5\mathrm{-}50^{\circ}$ . These copper–benzenecarboxylate units form a face-centered crystal lattice of Fm3m symmetry, which has a complex three–dimensional channel system as depicted in Figure 5A. The main pores of ca. $0.9{\mathrm{nm}}$ in diameter form a cubic network that is well seen from the [100] direction. In addition, planar $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{6}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{C})_{3}$ segments form tetrahedron shaped side pockets. The window of these pockets is ca. 0.35 nm in diameter, which is wide enough for small molecules of gases to pass through, but it is too tight for larger organic molecules [37].  In MOF Cu-BTC, the triangular shaped coordination of the trimesic acid plays an important role in the linkage of the nods resulting in a three-dimensional honeycomb framework (Figure 5A). Cu–BTC is also based on a binuclear $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ unit, in which Cu(II) centers are coordinated to four carboxylate oxygen atoms  from two fully deprotonated $\mathbf{BTC}^{3-}$ ligands, and one oxygen atom from the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ligand (Figure 5B). The distance between two Cu#1 centers is $2.628\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ [25, 37].
